{"title":"llm-gemini 0.38","link":"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/18/llm-gemini/","date":1795046360000,"content":"\n    \n        <p><strong>Release:</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/simonw/llm-gemini/releases/tag/0.38\">llm-gemini 0.38</a></p>\n        <p>Today Google <a href=\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/introducing-gemini-4-neon/\">introduced Gemini 4 Neon</a>. I <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/12/llm-gemini/\">wrote recently</a> about how confusing the pricing for the Gemini 4 series was - Neon has somehow made this even worse.</p>\n<p>Previously the Gemini 4 lineup was Hydrogen, Argon, and Carbon - where Argon and Carbon were <em>the same price</em>, and Hydrogen was 3/4 of that price now but will be 1/4 of that price when the other two double an 1st December once their initial discount pricing expires.</p>\n<p>The Neon announcement pretends that Argon doesn't exist. Here's their new image explaining the model line-up:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Comparison table slide titled &quot;Gemini 4 - Our next era of frontier intelligence&quot; with three model columns, Carbon, Neon and Hydrogen, where the Neon column is highlighted with a blue outline. Best For: Carbon - Hardest long-horizon projects and deep research; Neon - Deep reasoning across software engineering, knowledge work, and science; Hydrogen - Speed and efficiency in everyday coding and agentic workflows. Quality (Artificial Analysis Index): Carbon 60, Neon 61, Hydrogen 54. Price ($/1M In / Out (1M flat)): Carbon $4 / $20*, Neon $3 / $15, Hydrogen $1.50 / $7.50. Thinking levels: Carbon - Low, medium, high; Neon - Low, medium, high, xhigh; Hydrogen - Low, medium, high, xhigh. Speed (shown as rocket icons out of five): Carbon 2, Neon 3.5, Hydrogen 5. Subscriptions: Carbon - Google AI Ultra; Neon - Google AI Pro &amp; Ultra; Hydrogen - For Everyone. Availability: Carbon - Gemini API in Google AI Studio (paid), Gemini for business, Google AI Ultra: Gemini app, Google Antigravity, AI Mode in Search; Neon - Gemini API in Google AI Studio (paid), Gemini for business, Google AI Pro &amp; Ultra: Gemini app, Google Antigravity (paid); Hydrogen - Gemini API in Google AI Studio (all tiers), Gemini for business, Gemini app, Google Search, Google AI Pro &amp; Ultra: Google Antigravity (paid). Footnote: *$2 / $10 promo through Nov 30. Dated 18 November 2026.\" src=\"/media/3b5a757b14d56636a832cb54c6fc6a26f1bf8a7fe964db290addc91dd0b3dbb7.webp\" /></p>\n<p>So the lineup now, from most to least expensive, is Carbon, Neon, Hydrogen.</p>\n<p>Except Carbon is currently half price, so right now Neon ($3/million input, $15/million output) is the most expensive of the three - then Carbon ($2/$10), then Hydrogen ($1.50/$7.50). I guess this will make more sense when Carbon doubles to $4/$20 on December 1st.</p>\n<p>I ran <a href=\"https://tools.simonwillison.net/markdown-svg-renderer?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgist.github.com%2Fsimonw%2Fabc5dfe18e8d37a46fd09a3dcc18ed5c\">some pelicans</a> through the three reasoning levels for Neon - low, medium, high.</p>\n<p>I dropped Argon from <a href=\"https://static.simonwillison.net/static/2026/gemini-4-carbon-neon-hydrogen.html\">my Gemini 4 comparison grid</a> - here's the new Gemini 4 lineup, where the Neon pelicans do look better than Hydrogen and worse than Carbon:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Comparison grid of nine flat vector-style illustrations of a pelican riding a red bicycle, arranged as a table with three model columns and three thinking-level rows. Column headers: Thinking, gemini-4-carbon, gemini-4-neon, gemini-4-hydrogen. Row HIGH: gemini-4-carbon is the most detailed of all, a pelican in a sailor cap on a red cruiser bike with fenders, chain guard and a wicker basket holding a fish, on a beach promenade with ocean, sun, clouds and distant birds, captioned &quot;40 input tokens, 20,305 output = 20.31 cents · 4m 11s&quot;; gemini-4-neon is a clean, well-proportioned pelican on a simpler red bike on a road with a yellow dashed line and the sea behind, captioned &quot;40 input tokens, 9,691 output = 14.55 cents · 1m 11s&quot;; gemini-4-hydrogen is a plainer pelican on a basic red bike with speed lines, flat green ground and a pale sky, captioned &quot;40 input tokens, 5,311 output = 3.99 cents · 19s&quot;. Row MEDIUM: gemini-4-carbon adds a red striped scarf blowing in the wind plus the sailor cap, cream fenders, a basket and a beach with a rope fence, captioned &quot;40 input tokens, 11,020 output = 11.03 cents · 1m 53s&quot;; gemini-4-neon has a basket with a fish, green hills and a grey road, captioned &quot;40 input tokens, 7,846 output = 11.78 cents · 54s&quot;; gemini-4-hydrogen is a simple pelican and bike on a flat green strip with faint clouds, captioned &quot;40 input tokens, 3,964 output = 2.98 cents · 17s&quot;. Row LOW: gemini-4-carbon is still richly detailed with cap, scarf, basket, a seaside railing and a road, captioned &quot;40 input tokens, 9,153 output = 9.16 cents · 1m 34s&quot;; gemini-4-neon is a simpler pelican on a bike with no basket, on a road edged with grass, captioned &quot;40 input tokens, 4,974 output = 7.47 cents · 43s&quot;; gemini-4-hydrogen is the most minimal, a basic pelican and thin-framed bike over a single green line on a plain pale background, captioned &quot;40 input tokens, 2,996 output = 2.25 cents · 10s&quot;. Overall, detail and polish drop from carbon to neon to hydrogen and, less sharply, from high to low thinking, while every image remains a recognisable pelican on a bicycle.\" src=\"/media/329a0cfd0193291162a351866aff097a97273ed015ca590a47380b53f15006e7.webp\" /></p>\n<p>As I've done with <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/yonkers-rising/\">several recent models</a>, I also fired the following prompt against Gemini 4 Neon on \"extra\" effort level in Google Antigravity:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>Make a three js animation where yonkers stands up as a big mech (cool stand up motion) and stomps around manhattan and queens and Brooklyn, use openstreetmap mapping data</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><a href=\"https://static.simonwillison.net/static/2026/gemini-4-neon-yonkers/index.html\">Here's the result</a>.</p>\n\n  \n\n    \n    \n        <p>Tags: <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/yonkers-rising\">yonkers-rising</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/gemini\">gemini</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/llm\">llm</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/pelican-riding-a-bicycle\">pelican-riding-a-bicycle</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/ai\">ai</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/llms\">llms</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/llm-release\">llm-release</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/google\">google</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/generative-ai\">generative-ai</a></p>\n    \n\n","author":"Simon Willison","siteTitle":"Simon Willison’s Weblog","siteHash":"1b599216bd27a00f15d67a3ed9719c64148630c70244b6dd8e743f17cb45527e","entryHash":"1f6e235de78da043f627994a39d8692f8c165d72cdff1aab053773bff47b5128","category":"Tech"}